<h2><a id="Presentació" name="Presentació"></a>Presentation and options</h2>
<p>This program determines the optimum routes between a set of origin locations and a set of possible destination locations. The optimum route between a pair of origin-destination locations minimizes movement from the origin to the destination, whether in terms of distance, time, or another user-defined numeric field representing movement cost. The program offers four options:</p>
<!--Aquí inicia la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->
<span id="wpsopcions"/>
<!--Aquí acaba la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA editable de el WPS.-->

  <p>The origin locations and the possible destination locations should be connected to the network that defines the set of possible routes. This means that they correspond to nodes of the MiraMon arc/node topology. The type of location is determined by the values of a field of the node database (*N.dbf) that the user must create and fill out (if this information is in a point layer it can be transferred to the node layer using the PNT-&gt;NOD mode of the <a href="AtriTop.htm">AtriTop</a> application). The values accepted in this field are:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>&quot;O&quot;, for the origin nodes.</li>

    <li>&quot;D&quot;, for the destination nodes.</li>

    <li style="list-style: none"></li>

    <li>&quot;A&quot;, in both cases, for the origin and destination nodes at the same time.</li>

    <li>&quot;&quot;, blank for the nodes that are neither origin nor destination.</li>
  </ul>The arcs determine the direct connections between nodes, and their database (*A.dbf) must include fields that define the costs associated with each connection. Regarding distance, the topological ellipsoidal length field is used whenever it is present and the user does not specify an alternative field; otherwise the planimetric length field is used (both fields should exist by default in the arc database). If the user provides a custom field, its values are checked to be valid numeric values greater than zero; otherwise the program stops execution.

  <p>The resulting file is a table in <b>DBF</b> format that establishes a relationship between each origin node and a destination node that optimizes its path (provided there is at least one succession of arcs connecting the origin node and the destination node and its accumulated cost is less than the maximum cost specified on the command line). Note that the same destination node may be the end of several optimum routes, or of none.</p>

  <p>The costs of each optimum route found are displayed on the screen and saved to the DBF table specified when calling the program. The resulting values are expressed in the same units as the fields involved (for example, when a speed field in <i>km/h</i> is used, accumulated distances are given in <i>km</i> and accumulated times in hours). For the results to be calculated correctly, the fields involved must have their units set in the metadata and these must be recognized by MiraMon. Contact us if you wish to use units not recognized by MiraMon.</p>

